  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracts in Maryland totaled $34.6B in FY2026 to date. The federal government made 83,136 awards in that period, with an average award size of $416K. Obligations fell 12.9% year over year. The Department of Defense was the largest buyer, obligating $13.4B, and the Department of Health and Human Services followed with $7.2B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences was the largest industry by obligation at $3.9B, and Other Computer Related Services, Engineering Services, and Computer Systems Design Services each exceeded $3.4B. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC led vendors with $1.8B in obligations, and ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC and AMERICAN CENTRIFUGE OPERATING, LLC each received $900M. Past fiscal years show higher totals, including $46.3B in FY24 and $39.7B in FY25, providing context for the current-year decline. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What explains the FY2026 to date decline in obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 12.9% year over year, which reduced the FY2026 to date total to $34.6B. Program-level shifts mattered: the Department of Defense obligated $13.4B and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $7.2B, but those amounts combined were lower than prior peak years such as FY24 at $46.3B. Award volume and size also play a role: there were 83,136 awards with an average award size of $416K. Procurement in high-dollar industries like Research and Development and Computer Services continued, but did not offset the overall drop."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, vendors, and industries dominated Maryland obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense was the top buyer with $13.4B, followed by the Department of Health and Human Services with $7.2B. THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC received the most vendor obligations at $1.8B, and ORANO FEDERAL SERVICES LLC and AMERICAN CENTRIFUGE OPERATING, LLC each received $900M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences led industries at $3.9B, with Other Computer Related Services at $3.6B and Engineering Services at $3.5B."
      }
    ]
  },
